//// 1.0.10-beta `00 · 05-wasm` — **how a `?T` CARRIES a `T` that is already a
//// pointer**, and every reader that has to agree with it.
////
//// wasm is the only backend with a carrier to get wrong: a `?T` is an i32
//// offset and `0` is absence (decision 3), so a scalar payload goes in a box
//// and a pointer payload — a string, a record, an array — is its own offset.
//// The two decisions were taken in two places: `lowerArrayMethod` picked the
//// helper (`$__arr_at` or `$__arr_at_box`) and `optInfoOf` reported the shape,
//// and a RECORD element fell between them, because a record shares the `i32`
//// element slot with an integer. So `es.at(0)` was written boxed and read as a
//// bare pointer, and every line below answered a heap address at exit 0 where
//// the other three backends answered a value — `es.at(0)?.key.length()` was
//// `276` against `3`, and the narrowed `val k: string = first.key` was `272`
//// against `abc`. Exit 0 throughout, with nothing said: which is why every
//// line here asserts the VALUE.
////
//// Green on all four targets. The shapes, in order: printing the optional
//// whole; `?.` on a field, and on a field a primitive method then reads; the
//// name a `!= null` test narrows (`00 · 01-checker`'s rule, read here through
//// wasm's carrier — `run/narrowing_null_check.bp` is where the CHECKER's half
//// is pinned); `??` with a record default, whose result is a record and whose
//// field is a declared `string`; the same `.at()` reached through a record
//// FIELD declared `Entry[]`, which is where the element's shape has to come
//// from the field's declared type; and a `?T[]` — an element that is itself a
//// pointer for the same reason a record is.
////
//// NOT here, and deliberately: `@print` of an ABSENT optional. Every backend
//// spells absence differently today (`null` on commonJS and wasm, the atom
//// `undefined` on erlang and beam) and decision 47's row owns that
//// disagreement — `run/index_at_optional.bp` carries it. This cell reads the
//// absent value through `??` instead, which every backend agrees on. Nor is
//// `.toString()` after a `?.` chain (`es.at(1)?.key.length().toString()`):
//// that is `run/optional_chain_method.bp`'s, a gap of its own and not the
//// carrier's.

type Entry(key: string) {}
type Table(rows: Entry[]) {}

pub fn main() {
    val es = [Entry(key: "abc"), Entry(key: "de")];

    // The optional printed whole: present, it is the record's own text.
    @print(es.at(0));

    // `?.` on a field, and a primitive method on what `?.` answered.
    @print(es.at(0)?.key);
    @print(es.at(1)?.key.length());

    // The name a `!= null` test narrows: inside the branch it is the payload.
    val first = es.at(0);
    if (first != null) {
        val k: string = first.key;
        @print(k);
        @print(first.key.length());
    };

    // `??` with a record default — the result is a record, and its field is a
    // declared `string`.
    @print((es.at(9) ?? Entry(key: "zz")).key);

    // The same `.at()` through a record FIELD declared `Entry[]`.
    val t = Table(rows: es);
    @print(t.rows.at(1)?.key);
    @print(t.rows.first()?.key.length());

    // An element that is itself an array is a pointer for the same reason.
    val grid = [[1, 2, 3], [4]];
    val row = grid.at(0);
    if (row != null) { @print(row.length()); };
}
